{
  "_note": "Component SERIES registry — versioned DATA, the standards-table half of docs/backlog/se-off-the-shelf-fabrication.md engine 1. A `component` ref is one SKU; hand-entering 400 screws is not viable, so a series carries a family plus its VALID-COMBINATION size table (M6x2 does not exist; DN50 wall thicknesses are a fixed list) and an entity is minted from a series row on first use. Deliberately a file, not a table: these are published standards dimensions, not agent-authored rows — same posture as pcb_capabilities.json (curated, versioned, diffable, no migration to change a number). Runtime rows the mint produces are ordinary component_spec_values with method='standard' and the series `source` as provenance. Every numeric value here is MILLIMETRES, one unit for the whole file (a per-value unit column on curated data is a place for a typo to hide). The mint converts to each spec's canonical_unit on the way in — component_specs is mostly mm but `length_overall` is metres, so a 2000mm tube must not land as 2000m; see component_series.to_canonical and migration 0152's unit note. `sizes[].specs` are the per-size dimensions; `specs` at the series level apply to every size; `lengths` (present only where the series has a length variable) is the discrete length list, and a size key without one of these lengths is not a real part. `sizes[].stocking` is `universal | common | specialty` — a CURATED HOUSE JUDGEMENT of how widely that size is held, not a standard and not a live number: it is the offline prior a live supplier stock quote overrides (docs/backlog/se-off-the-shelf-fabrication.md, 'Stock as a selection signal'), and it exists so selection ranks M4x12 above M14x55 without a network. Confidence: the ISO fastener series are transcribed from the published dimension tables and are exact; the 2026-09-15 batch (10642, 7380, 14579, 14581, 14583, 14585, 14586, 4026, 7040, 4035, 7090) was transcribed from the FreeCAD Fasteners workbench standards tables and spot-checked against fasteners.eu, with ISO 14585/14586 drive assignments taken from the latter; en-10255-medium is the EN 10255 medium (M) series; acrylic-sheet-cast is a STOCK RANGE, not a standard — it is what European cast-acrylic suppliers commonly hold, and it carries no designation for that reason; insert-brass-heatset has NO standard at all (a supplier form) and says so in its own source line. A COUNTERSUNK series carries the THEORETICAL sharp-cone `head_diameter` and `head_angle`, and no `head_height`: the sink depth is (head_diameter − thread nominal)/2 by geometry, so transcribing it again would be a second place for the same number to be wrong.",
